      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "823bfe19-376f-54cc-8fa9-59a8ce25fc7a",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Hawkins County, Sullivan County, Washington County, Claiborne County, Hancock County, Lee County, Virginia, Tennessee, Cumberland Gap, Kentucky, Washington, Pennsylvania, Ohio Valley, Ohio\n- People: Henry Clay\n- Routes: Wilderness Road, National Road\n- Topics: Greene–Jones War, Melungeon population\n\nFacts:\n- The Greene–Jones War took place primarily in border areas of Hawkins County, Washington County, Claiborne County, and Hancock County in Tennessee and Lee County in Virginia, near Cumberland Gap.\n- Sullivan County was part of North Carolina and was one of the earliest Tennessee counties.\n- Hawkins County was formerly part of Sullivan County and was said to have a large Melungeon population.\n- Hancock County, Tennessee, was created when Hawkins County was subdivided.\n- Cumberland Gap was a narrow Appalachian Mountain pass, and the Wilderness Road led through it into Kentucky.\n- Early in the 19th century, Henry Clay advocated the Wilderness Road route.\n- Early in the 19th century, the northern route was selected instead for the National Road, connecting near Washington, Pennsylvania, into the Ohio Valley of northern Kentucky and Ohio.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "a1486a21-fae8-5850-b255-d8e62c1790f9",
        "text": "Page: Greene–Jones War. Sentence: The feud took place primarily in the border areas of Hawkins County (formerly part of Sullivan County, North Carolina, one of the earliest Tennessee counties and said to have a large Melungeon population), Washington County, Claiborne County, Hancock County, Tennessee (created when Hawkins County, Tennessee was subdivided), and Lee County, Virginia, not far from Cumberland Gap, the narrow Appalachian Mountain pass sometimes called The Wilderness Road leading into Kentucky.\nPage: Wilderness Road. Sentence: Despite Kentucky Senator Henry Clay's advocacy of this route, early in the 19th century, the northern route was selected for the National Road, connecting near Washington, Pennsylvania into the Ohio Valley of northern Kentucky and Ohio.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
